   plain='Total liabilities have two parts: borrowing that sits in the Consolidated Fund (public debt) and money held in trust in the Public Account (provident funds and the like). Public debt can be internal (owed at home) or external (owed to foreigners). The nation’s external debt is bigger because it adds private borrowing abroad. Good debt management: cheap, long, rupee-based, home-sourced, and with the interest rate below the growth rate. If interest runs above growth, the debt ratio snowballs even with no new deficit.',
